<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ludwig Wittgenstein</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1889">1889</a>–<a href="/years/1951">1951</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Wittgenstein</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ludwig Wittgenstein was a philosopher born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> in 1889 into one of the wealthiest families of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire</a>. He became one of the most influential philosophers of the twentieth century, working mainly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>. He is often cited as part of the intellectual culture of late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> Vienna.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ludwig-wittgenstein.jpg" alt="Ludwig Wittgenstein" width="310" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Moritz Nähr · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ludwig_Wittgenstein_1929.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ludwig Josef Johann Wittgenstein was born on 26 April 1889 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>. His father, Karl Wittgenstein, meant his sons to become captains of industry and had them educated at home; three of the five brothers later took their own lives. After the disappearance and presumed suicide of Hans in 1902, Karl allowed the two youngest sons to attend school, and in 1903 Ludwig entered the Realschule in Linz. From 1906 he studied mechanical engineering in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>. Wanting to study with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gottlob-frege">Gottlob Frege</a>, he followed Frege's advice and arrived unannounced at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bertrand-russell">Bertrand Russell</a>'s rooms at Trinity College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, on 18 October 1911.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After Karl's death in January 1913 he inherited one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>'s largest fortunes and gave much of it to poets and artists. At the outbreak of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> he volunteered for the Austro-Hungarian Army. In 1916 he was posted to the Russian front, where he directed artillery fire from an observation post and was awarded the Military Merit Medal with Swords on the Ribbon. He finished the Tractatus Logico-Philosophicus in August 1918; it appeared in German in 1921 and in English in 1922. In the 1920s he taught in Austrian village schools, where his use of corporal punishment caused controversy.</p><p>He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a> in 1929, offered the Tractatus as his doctoral thesis, and in 1939 was elected to the chair of philosophy that G. E. Moore had resigned. He became a British subject on 12 April 1939. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> he worked as a porter at Guy's Hospital in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, and he came back to Cambridge for the last time in October 1944.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>The Tractatus is concerned with the logical relationship between propositions and the world. Wittgenstein held that what can be said by propositions must be separated from what can only be shown. It influenced the Vienna Circle, although he was never a member.</p><p>His later thought, set out mainly in the Philosophical Investigations, published in 1953 after his death on 29 April 1951, rejected many assumptions of the Tractatus. He asked readers to think of language as a multiplicity of language games and argued that, in a large class of cases, the meaning of a word is its use in the language. His papers amount to an estimated 20,000 pages.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ludwig Wittgenstein” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…intellectual capitals of the Western world, producing the foundations of psychoanalysis (Freud), political Zionism (Herzl), the art of the Secession and Expressionism (Klimt, Schiele, Kokoschka), the Second Viennese School of music (Schoenberg, Berg, Webern), and the Austrian school of economics (Menger), while nurturing thinkers such as <b>Ludwig Wittgenstein</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Russians under General Peter <b>Wittgenstein</b> defeated the French forces of Marshal Claude Victor and Marshal Nicholas Oudinot at Smoliani.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-14">14 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Retreating from Moscow, Napoleon's Grande Armée began fighting its way across the Berezina River, in present-day Belarus, under attack by Russian forces under <b>Wittgenstein</b> and Chichagov.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-26">26 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>After Henry III's death the lands passed through his sister to the Sponheim family, forming Sayn and Sayn-<b>Wittgenstein</b> lines.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-sayn">County of Sayn</a></li><li><blockquote>The County of Sayn-<b>Wittgenstein</b> was divided in 1605 among the sons of Count Ludwig I. Georg received Berleburg in the northern Wittgenstein land, founding a line that would persist alongside Sayn-Wittgenstein-Hohenstein.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-sayn-wittgenstein-berleburg">County of Sayn-Wittgenstein-Berleburg</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Neuwaldegg, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.24,16.29&amp;year=1929" aria-label="Neuwaldegg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4928 476 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ludwig Wittgenstein"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5378 746h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5378 746h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.24,16.29&amp;year=1929">What was here in 1929? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
